  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough next Wednesday...\n\nDeep monsoonal moisture continues to lift out of the great basin\nand mesoanalysis shows pwats of 1.10-1.20\" across the region.\nDespite the anomalously high moisture, \"cooler\" low level east \nwinds keep a mid level cap in place at lower elevations thru much \nof the day. Convection will develop over the mountains by 19-20z\n(there is some beginning upstream near the Tetons). CAMs show\nconvection struggling to spread off the foothills even by this\nevening, but given weak synoptic ascent and expectation of outflow\nboundaries within this very moist air mass, there should be some\nactivity of lesser coverage spread over the lower elevations. Main\nrisk of today's storms is of course heavy rain capable of\nproducing flash flooding, and this includes burn scars such as\nRobertson Draw and East Side near Red Lodge, and Elk along the\neastern slopes of the Bighorns. A Flood Watch remains in effect\nfrom 1pm to 2am. Expect activity in the south to go thru late\nevening and even into the overnight hours, per slowly tracking\nshortwave across northern WY.\n\nShowers and weak t-storms should linger into early Thursday over\nour S/SE, but we will see large scale subsidence spread over the\narea behind the departing wave. As a result, CAMs and lower\nresolution models have trended drier for tomorrow. However, pwats\nwill remain anomalously high for another day (still >1\") and feel\nthe potential for a few diurnal showers and weak t-storms is a bit\nunderdone by the models. Temps into the lower 90s really should \nrelease some surface-based instability.\n\nFriday will be hotter and drier w/ breezy mixed west winds, due to\nridge flattening from a weak shortwave energy in southern Canada.\nThis is looking like the first of several days of hotter & drier \nweather supported by a strong stagnating ridge over the \nintermountain west. Ensembles and cluster analyses show high \nconfidence here. Look for above normal temps through at least the\nmiddle of next week, meaning highs generally ranging from 95-105F\neach day through next Wednesday. Monsoon moisture spreading thru \nthe ridge should yield a low risk (10-20% chance) of diurnal high-\nbased t-storms each day. In fact, today's models have trended more\nsupportive of daily convection. Any storms will be non-severe but\ncould produce erratic winds, and with pwats still in the \n0.75-1.00\" range could also cause localized moderate rainfall. \nImpacts from Friday into next week will be twofold. Heat related \nimpacts will be of concern with the triple digit highs and warm \nnighttime lows offering little relief. Also, with min RHs expected\nin the low-mid teens and poor nighttime recoveries, fire concerns\nwill also increase over time especially in areas missing out on \nrainfall this week. Fire concerns will be exacerbated by periods \nof breezy mixed winds along with a daily risk of lightning. This \nwill be something to monitor over the coming days. The 8-14 day \noutlook from CPC shows high confidence in warmer than normal \ntemperatures persisting into early August.\n\nFinally a comment on this Saturday, July 25th...the only day in\nJuly on which the Billings airport has not reached 100F in its\nperiod of record (back to 1934). Smoke shouldn't be a factor as\nthe SW flow takes PacNW smoke to our north. However, the ridge is\nlooking a bit dirtier, and in fact there is confidence in water \nvapor transport in the afternoon leading to some diurnal \nconvection over our west. Thus, increasing cloud cover could limit\ntemperatures by mid-late afternoon. Current forecast at KBIL is \n103F. Stay tuned.\n\nJKL",
